From 3b2ca4c78f2e6ed6fea3891bfe052a3d804b2834 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Avery Felts <averyfelts@Averys-MacBook-Air.local>
Date: Fri, 23 Oct 2026 21:55:10 -0600
Subject: [PATCH 24/25] MSL: cull distances as clip distances

Metal has no cull distances. With cull_distance_as_clip_distance, the rasterizing stage (vertex, tessellation
evaluation, geometry or mesh shader) writes each as a clip distance: the cull distance where it is negative,
1e20 elsewhere, so a primitive whose vertices all have a negative one is clipped away and any other is clipped
at its negative vertices only. Also: a geometry shader's outputs that go to the stage output struct at the end
of the entry point (clip and cull distances of the gl_PerVertex block) go there for each EmitVertex; it reads
clip and cull distance arrays of gl_in from the payload element by element; a mesh shader's per-vertex output
doesn't write a cull distance array the per-vertex struct doesn't have; and the vertex wrapper of a geometry
pipeline doesn't pass ViewIndex (the vertex function derives it).
---
